From 3bc7668089272b0d1453264ddd811116e1baf7f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:54:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kk: compile a pipeline's programs on a thread pool

Creating a pipeline compiles the Metal pipelines of its programs, and until
now each of them was compiled on the thread that created the pipeline. A title
creates its pipelines from many threads, but their work is drained by the
virtio-gpu ring thread of the Vulkan context they belong to, and a compile
blocks that ring thread for as long as Metal takes. On Dragon's Dogma 2's first
run that is the bulk of ~1,700 s of Metal compilation (MESA_KK_DEBUG=compiles
over the whole run), spent with the guest blocked on the game's "compiling
shaders" screen.

Queue a program at creation and compile it on a pool of threads instead, and
wait where its Metal pipeline is first used:

  - kk_compile_shaders() and kk_deserialize_shader() enqueue programs
    (kk_compile_enqueue). min(16, cores - 2) threads take them and call
    kk_compile_program (kk_compile_pool_init, kk_compile_worker); the count
    can be set with MESA_KK_COMPILE_THREADS.
  - kk_cmd_shader_pipeline() waits for the program a draw or dispatch uses and
    kk_cmd_bind_graphics_shader() for the vertex program whose depth/stencil
    state a bind reads (kk_compile_wait), so a draw that needs a program still
    being compiled waits for it instead of drawing nothing.
  - kk_shader_destroy() waits for a compile that is still writing into the
    shader before releasing its pipeline handles.
  - The device's compiled-library table is now shared by the pool: its lock
    guards the table only. Holding it across the Metal compile would make the
    pool compile one library at a time, which is what the pool exists to
    avoid.

Two things are required once a compile can fail after creation:

  - A 64-bit atomic operation Metal cannot express (there is no atomic_long and
    no threadgroup 64-bit atomic) has no MSL at all, so it has to keep failing
    at pipeline creation, where the application can skip the pipeline:
    nir_uses_unsupported_64bit_atomics() rejects such a shader with
    VK_ERROR_INVALID_SHADER_NV. Without it the failure would only surface in a
    background compile that creation already returned from.
  - A compile that fails later is reported on the command buffer and the draw
    or dispatch that needs it is dropped, instead of binding a nil pipeline
    (Metal validation aborts on that).

Dragon's Dogma 2, M5 Max, cold Metal cache and no guest shader cache: the
title screen is up at 17 minutes with the compilation continuing in the
background, where the same first run previously spent 62 minutes with the
player watching the game's compile screen. The remaining floor is Apple's
compiler service, which runs the compiles on about one core
(MTLCompilerService in top) however many are submitted.

